\name{BaseRBiplotLayerProcessor}
\alias{BaseRBiplotLayerProcessor}
\title{Base R Biplot Processor}
\description{
Reads \code{biplot()} as the two things it draws: the observations in principal
component space, and the variables' loadings on the same components.

\strong{It is a grid, and for a reason the other grids do not have.} \code{pairs()},
\code{lag.plot()} and \code{termplot()} are read as grids because they draw several
panels. A biplot draws its two halves \emph{on top of each other} -- but on
\strong{two different pairs of axes}, which is the whole trick of the chart.
Measured, the two \code{plot.xy} calls run over different ranges, and the
quantities behind them are different sizes again:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{scores   PC1 range  -1.716 ..  2.199
loadings PC1 range  -0.962 .. -0.012
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Announcing both against one axis pair would misstate every loading. So the
two are given a cell each -- one row, two columns -- which is the only way
the existing grammar can say "these have separate scales" without
inventing a second axis on one layer.

\strong{It draws no points at all.} Both \code{plot.xy} calls are \code{type = "n"};
every mark on the page is a \emph{label} or an \emph{arrow}. Measured on the export
of a ten-observation, four-variable fit:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{graphics-plot-1-text-1     10 children   the observations
graphics-plot-2-text-1      4 children   the variables
graphics-plot-2-arrows-1    4 children   the arrows
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Both text grobs are addressable per datum, in data order, which is the
shape \code{lag.plot()}'s labelled panels already established -- one \code{g} per
label rather than one \code{use} per symbol. The arrows are not emitted
separately: an arrow and its label name the same variable and sit at the
same place, so the label is the mark a reader is moved to.

\strong{The values are the caller's, not the drawing's.} \code{biplot()} apportions
the two halves between the axes so that both fit one page -- it divides
the scores by \code{sdev * sqrt(n)} and multiplies the loadings by it, so
\emph{neither} set is drawn at its own scale. What a reader wants is the pair
that mean something: the \strong{scores}, an observation's coordinate in
component space, and the \strong{loadings}, a variable's weight on each
component. Those are announced. This is the same choice \code{stars()} makes,
where the radii on the page are shares of a column's range and the reading
hands over the readings instead.
}
